Z||000008||loren||03-08-2001||10:17 AM||||thanks for the comeback fieroshop...i sure would appreciate a detailed account of how to get a v6 timed by hand and ear...i will forever be in your debt if you can get me on the right track.<BR>also thanks to all who have given me info.<BR>loren<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Oreif||03-08-2001||10:27 AM||||Setting timing by ear isn't a good idea unless, like George, You have experience or an ear for it. Usually setting timing by ear if you are not experienced with it will cause you to set the timing too high. Did you short out the ALDL terminal? As for finding TDC, You have to remember that there are 2 times that the piston hits TDC.(4-cycle engine) The distributor may not be pointing towards the #1 wire during the #1 cylinder exhaust/intake strokes. Basically I use a compression tester. The timing mark on the pulley should should be at "0" when piston is at TDC. So I hook up a compression gage, When I start seeing the gage move up in pressure I know its on it's compression stroke. Remove compression gage and set crank to "0" on the timing mark. Next place a screwdriver in the spark hole and move the crank pulley back and forth a little. The piston should be at it's highest spot at "0". This will verify the crank pulley and the piston are set correctly. Once that is done see where the distributor is set. It should be just about to hit the #1 pole for the #1 spark plug. If you move the crank to 10* it should be just on the #1. If it is not it needs to be set to it. At this point put everything together, short pins 1 & 2 of the ALDL, start car and set timing to 10*.<BR>Also you say you changed the pick-up coil, Did you change the ignition module too? Why did you change the pick-up coil? I had my pick-up coil die on me so I changed everything just to be safe.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||loren||03-08-2001||08:12 PM||||thanks: stugood,will,rogergarrison,fieroshop,<BR>tgowens,oreif,fierocd,c m west,davidhenshaw,<BR>indiana resto guy,(hope i havent missed anyone)<BR>my car (85 gt 6 cyl) had a history of having to be towed (by previous owner) so i decided to replace the ignition module and pickup coil....so far, it  starts and runs well.in fact it is running real good, but the timing mark on the damper is about 1 inch to the left of where it should be(according to a timing light). if i put a jumper on the diagnostic plug and time for 10 deg.btc the engine runs sluggish and smells like it is trying to diesel and backs off at most any speed when i let off of the accelerator .<BR>i will take the advise that you guys have provided and start the process all over again<BR>saturday 4/9<BR>thanks loren<BR>||||reg||1
